# ${PROJECT_NAME} — AI workspace

The one instruction file for this AI workspace. Claude Code 2.1.277 or later loads it when the
workspace has no `CLAUDE.md`; an older Claude Code reads only `CLAUDE.md` and sees none of this.
Codex, OMP and other agents read `AGENTS.md` directly.
This is a temporary stub: ossify 1.24.0 or later replaces a file that starts with the marker above.
An older ossify reads it as an existing file and asks you to keep it or move it aside, and writes a
`CLAUDE.md` — install 1.24.0 or later before you run `/ossify:start` if you want the one-file result.
Put lasting notes in it only after `/ossify:start` has run.

This is the AI workspace half of a dual-repo project.
Paired canonical repo: `${CANONICAL_ROOT}`.

## What workspace-init declared

- `.workspace/pairing.json` — pairing manifest and repository topology (see its `routing` block)
- `.workspace/init-log` — workspace-init's rollback record
- `AGENTS.md` and `README.md` — temporary stubs

Production source, tests, product documentation and product ADRs belong in the canonical.
Sprint specs, the memory bank and process records belong here. PRDs and SRSs also go to the
canonical — see the manifest's `routing` block for the full table.

## Continue with ossify

- Empty canonical: run `/ossify:start` (on Codex or OMP, the `start` skill).
- `/ossify:adopt` currently targets projects previously onboarded with the legacy scaffold stack — a canonical that already carries source or history without that stack has no supported ossify continuation yet. On ossify 1.24.0 or later, adopt merges its loop section into this `AGENTS.md`. On an older ossify it merges that section into `CLAUDE.md` instead, which then shadows `AGENTS.md` — merge it into `AGENTS.md` by hand afterwards, then remove `CLAUDE.md`.

## Git policy

- Network ops (push/pull/fetch) require explicit user instruction. Default-deny per the manifest's `git_policy.allow_ai_*` fields.
- Local merges and rebases are allowed.

## AI-trace filter

A `commit-msg` hook is installed in both this AI workspace and the canonical repo. It blocks:
- `Co-Authored-By:` trailers
- `🤖 Generated with` markers
- `<noreply@anthropic.com>` / `<noreply@openai.com>` references

Override with `git commit --no-verify` only if you understand the implication.
